WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:29.960
I'm your school bell ding dong ding, boys and girls all hear me.

00:29.960 --> 00:39.960
Every time I ding dong ding, come with me to play and sing.

00:39.960 --> 00:47.960
Ding dong ding dong ding.

00:47.960 --> 00:50.960
Good morning. What day is it?

00:50.960 --> 00:55.960
You're right, Friday. Yes, what are you going to do?

00:55.960 --> 01:03.960
Are you? Good. Tomorrow too?

01:03.960 --> 01:10.960
Oh, I hope it's a nice day and you can play outdoors and you get a good rest.

01:10.960 --> 01:13.960
Yes, and maybe you'll be able to play outdoors again.

01:13.960 --> 01:16.960
Fine, you'll have lots of fun.

01:16.960 --> 01:19.960
I want to ask you a question.

01:19.960 --> 01:22.960
Have you ever been to the dentist?

01:22.960 --> 01:28.960
Do you know who a dentist is? Who?

01:28.960 --> 01:34.960
Right, he's the man who fixes your teeth.

01:34.960 --> 01:38.960
He does, he has a chair that's something like the barber's chair.

01:38.960 --> 01:44.960
When you crawl into it, climb up, crawl in, what happens?

01:44.960 --> 01:48.960
He makes it get higher and higher and higher and higher. That's right.

01:48.960 --> 01:55.960
You're right. Sometimes it does hurt a little, but when you have to have something fixed,

01:55.960 --> 01:58.960
it doesn't make any difference, does it? No.

01:58.960 --> 02:03.960
So, if you've never been to the dentist yet, when the time comes for you to go,

02:03.960 --> 02:07.960
you know who the dentist is and what he does.

02:07.960 --> 02:11.960
And then you'll know what to expect.

02:11.960 --> 02:17.960
When I woke up this morning, I thought of something that I wanted to do today.

02:17.960 --> 02:20.960
Something I haven't done for, oh, quite a while.

02:20.960 --> 02:22.960
I want to blow bubbles.

02:22.960 --> 02:27.960
I want to blow bubbles. And here's the table.

02:27.960 --> 02:29.960
Look at this.

02:29.960 --> 02:35.960
Yes, tubs with water, a jar with soap.

02:35.960 --> 02:37.960
Put some soap in.

02:37.960 --> 02:41.960
You like to blow bubbles, too. Do you?

02:41.960 --> 02:43.960
You're going to blow bubbles after a while.

02:43.960 --> 02:47.960
I know. I didn't tell you ahead of time we were going to blow bubbles today.

02:47.960 --> 02:50.960
Now I want to mix this soap all up.

02:50.960 --> 02:53.960
Yes, I use the bubble pipe just to mix it.

02:53.960 --> 02:55.960
Sure.

02:55.960 --> 02:59.960
Well, you do have to put enough soap in if you have a big tub like this.

02:59.960 --> 03:01.960
Why?

03:01.960 --> 03:04.960
You can't blow bubbles with just plain water.

03:04.960 --> 03:06.960
You can't do it.

03:06.960 --> 03:08.960
No.

03:08.960 --> 03:12.960
I wonder if I'll get big bubbles or little bubbles today.

03:12.960 --> 03:15.960
I don't know.

03:15.960 --> 03:17.960
You ready to watch?

03:17.960 --> 03:19.960
Really?

03:19.960 --> 03:23.960
Want me to blow hard or just a little?

03:23.960 --> 03:25.960
Blow slowly?

03:25.960 --> 03:27.960
All right.

03:27.960 --> 03:28.960
Here's the pipe.

03:28.960 --> 03:52.960
Let's see if I can get down slowly.

03:52.960 --> 03:56.960
Oh, I'm blowing lots of little bubbles.

03:56.960 --> 04:01.960
If you listen, you'll hear me blow bubbles.

04:01.960 --> 04:16.960
There, some of them fell off.

04:16.960 --> 04:44.960
Oh, I blew some bath bombs that time.

04:44.960 --> 05:03.960
That's a big one.

05:03.960 --> 05:04.960
Look at that.

05:04.960 --> 05:06.960
Oh, I burst.

05:06.960 --> 05:09.960
Let's try it again.

05:09.960 --> 05:11.960
Oh, I know.

05:11.960 --> 05:13.960
Yes, it does too, doesn't it?

05:13.960 --> 05:17.960
It takes a lot of breath to be able to blow bubbles.

05:17.960 --> 05:22.960
Sometimes you have to stop and take another breath.

05:22.960 --> 05:24.960
Sure.

05:24.960 --> 05:27.960
You have to be careful not to tilt the pipe back,

05:27.960 --> 05:30.960
because if you keep it right out straight, what happens?

05:30.960 --> 05:33.960
You get a mouthful of soapy water.

05:33.960 --> 05:35.960
Sure.

05:35.960 --> 06:04.960
Now let's see what happens.

06:04.960 --> 06:06.960
Oh, let's see.

06:06.960 --> 06:07.960
Shall we?

06:07.960 --> 06:09.960
Think we'll get a big one maybe this time?

06:09.960 --> 06:10.960
I don't know.

06:10.960 --> 06:33.960
Let's try it.

06:33.960 --> 06:57.960
Didn't get really very big ones, did we?

06:57.960 --> 07:00.960
No.

07:00.960 --> 07:03.960
Not a real big one that time.

07:03.960 --> 07:05.960
Maybe some other time we'll get a big one.

07:05.960 --> 07:09.960
Now when you're finished blowing bubbles, what happens?

07:09.960 --> 07:15.960
Yes, you always dry the bubble pipe.

07:15.960 --> 07:17.960
Why?

07:17.960 --> 07:21.960
So that it won't break.

07:21.960 --> 07:26.960
You dry it very carefully inside and outside.

07:26.960 --> 07:28.960
That's the important thing.

07:28.960 --> 07:30.960
Sure.

07:30.960 --> 07:32.960
Oh, that was fun.

07:32.960 --> 07:37.960
Sometimes to blow big bubbles and sometimes little bubbles.

07:37.960 --> 07:38.960
Sometimes they make noise.

07:38.960 --> 07:42.960
Sometimes they're very, very, very quiet.

07:42.960 --> 07:44.960
Yes.

07:44.960 --> 07:45.960
Did you hear that?

07:45.960 --> 07:48.960
You heard the noisy ones, but you didn't hear the quiet ones.

07:48.960 --> 07:50.960
No.

07:50.960 --> 07:56.960
Look who's right here.

07:56.960 --> 08:02.960
Well, all right here in the crib together.

08:02.960 --> 08:04.960
So many dogs.

08:04.960 --> 08:06.960
Good me.

08:06.960 --> 08:08.960
Are you comfortable in there?

08:08.960 --> 08:09.960
Are you?

08:09.960 --> 08:13.960
Well, I have a book with some poems.

08:13.960 --> 08:15.960
Oh, some of my favorite poems.

08:15.960 --> 08:17.960
And I want you to hear them.

08:17.960 --> 08:25.960
Do you remember when you sent a picture, a picture of a turtle, a big turtle?

08:25.960 --> 08:27.960
Oh, and you made one out of clay, too.

08:27.960 --> 08:29.960
Remember that?

08:29.960 --> 08:34.960
And I said we had a poem about a turtle, and that someday I would find it.

08:34.960 --> 08:36.960
Remember that?

08:36.960 --> 08:38.960
Oh, this is a wonderful poem.

08:38.960 --> 08:40.960
It was written by Rachel Lindsay.

08:40.960 --> 08:41.960
And it is such fun.

08:41.960 --> 08:42.960
Maybe you know it.

08:42.960 --> 08:44.960
If you know it, maybe you can say it with me.

08:44.960 --> 08:47.960
And it's called The Little Turtle.

08:47.960 --> 08:49.960
It's about a little turtle.

08:49.960 --> 08:52.960
There once was a little turtle.

08:52.960 --> 08:54.960
He lived in a box.

08:54.960 --> 08:56.960
He swam in a puddle.

08:56.960 --> 08:59.960
And he climbed on the rocks.

08:59.960 --> 09:01.960
He snapped at a mosquito.

09:01.960 --> 09:04.960
He snapped at a flea.

09:04.960 --> 09:06.960
He snapped at a minnow.

09:06.960 --> 09:09.960
And he snapped at me.

09:09.960 --> 09:11.960
He caught the mosquito.

09:11.960 --> 09:14.960
He caught the flea.

09:14.960 --> 09:15.960
He caught the minnow.

09:15.960 --> 09:17.960
But he didn't catch me.

09:17.960 --> 09:18.960
That's fun.

09:18.960 --> 09:20.960
Oh, I like that.

09:20.960 --> 09:23.960
Did you think that that mosquito was going to catch you?

09:23.960 --> 09:24.960
Did you really?

09:24.960 --> 09:27.960
Now, when you know Rachel Lindsay wrote another one.

09:27.960 --> 09:29.960
And this one is about the moon.

09:29.960 --> 09:31.960
The moon.

09:31.960 --> 09:32.960
Yes.

09:32.960 --> 09:34.960
Oh, and it has such an interesting title.

09:34.960 --> 09:38.960
The Moon, the North Wind's Cookie.

09:38.960 --> 09:41.960
The Moon, the North Wind's Cookie.

09:41.960 --> 09:42.960
Isn't that a funny one?

09:42.960 --> 09:45.960
You know what it means?

09:45.960 --> 09:50.960
Well, you remember we talked about crescents and a new moon?

09:50.960 --> 09:53.960
It was a tiny crescent, do you remember that?

09:53.960 --> 09:55.960
Well, and then we got a full moon.

09:55.960 --> 09:56.960
All right, well, now you watch.

09:56.960 --> 09:57.960
You listen.

09:57.960 --> 09:59.960
The moon's the north wind's cookie.

09:59.960 --> 10:06.960
He bites it day by day until there's but a rim of scraps that crumble all away.

10:06.960 --> 10:08.960
The south wind is a baker.

10:08.960 --> 10:18.960
He kneads clouds in his den and bakes a crisp new moon that greedy north wind eats again.

10:18.960 --> 10:20.960
That's an interesting one too, isn't it?

10:20.960 --> 10:23.960
Very interesting.

10:23.960 --> 10:24.960
We have one more.

10:24.960 --> 10:28.960
Oh, this one is lots and lots and lots of fun.

10:28.960 --> 10:30.960
Guess what the name of this one is.

10:30.960 --> 10:34.960
Oh, it's about something that you see all the time.

10:34.960 --> 10:36.960
It's called My Shadow.

10:36.960 --> 10:38.960
Did you ever see your own shadow?

10:38.960 --> 10:40.960
What does it look like?

10:40.960 --> 10:42.960
Sometimes is it short and fat?

10:42.960 --> 10:45.960
Sometimes is it tall and thin?

10:45.960 --> 10:47.960
Oh, this is about My Shadow.

10:47.960 --> 10:52.960
Listen, I have a little shadow that goes in and out with me.

10:52.960 --> 10:57.960
And what can be the use of him is more than I can see.

10:57.960 --> 11:02.960
He is very, very like me from the heels up to my head.

11:02.960 --> 11:08.960
And I see him jump before me when I jump into my bed.

11:08.960 --> 11:13.960
The funniest thing about him is the way he likes to grow.

11:13.960 --> 11:19.960
Not at all like proper children, which is always very slow.

11:19.960 --> 11:24.960
For he sometimes shoots up taller, like an India rubber ball.

11:24.960 --> 11:30.960
And he sometimes gets so little that there's none of him at all.

11:30.960 --> 11:34.960
He hasn't got a notion of how children ought to play.

11:34.960 --> 11:39.960
And can only make a fool of me in every sort of way.

11:39.960 --> 11:42.960
He stays so close beside me.

11:42.960 --> 11:44.960
And the color you can see.

11:44.960 --> 11:51.960
I think, I think shame to stick to nursey as that shadow sticks to me.

11:51.960 --> 11:55.960
One morning very early before the sun was up,

11:55.960 --> 12:00.960
I rose and found the shining dew on every buttercup.

12:00.960 --> 12:04.960
But my lazy little shadow, like an errant sleepyhead,

12:04.960 --> 12:11.960
had stayed at home behind me and was fast asleep in bed.

12:11.960 --> 12:14.960
Oh, shadows are lots of fun, aren't they?

12:14.960 --> 12:17.960
Yes, they are. Just lots and lots and lots of fun.

12:17.960 --> 12:20.960
Sure. You can play with your shadow.

12:20.960 --> 12:21.960
You can wave at your shadow.

12:21.960 --> 12:24.960
You can do all kinds of things with your shadow.

12:24.960 --> 12:26.960
Where have you ever seen yours?

12:26.960 --> 12:28.960
On the sidewalk?

12:28.960 --> 12:29.960
On the wall?

12:29.960 --> 12:30.960
Have you?

12:30.960 --> 12:31.960
On the garage door?

12:31.960 --> 12:32.960
Did you like that?

12:32.960 --> 12:35.960
Did you like that story about the shadow?

12:35.960 --> 12:36.960
Did you?

12:36.960 --> 12:39.960
You like all three of those ones?

12:39.960 --> 12:41.960
Oh, I think you know some of them.

12:41.960 --> 12:43.960
We'll have to say them again, won't we?

12:43.960 --> 12:46.960
Again and again and then we'll all learn them.

12:46.960 --> 12:48.960
Sure, it'd be fun to say those.

12:48.960 --> 12:49.960
We wouldn't need a book.

12:49.960 --> 12:51.960
No, we wouldn't need a book to read on it.

12:51.960 --> 12:53.960
Would you like that?

12:53.960 --> 12:55.960
Something else I want to show you.

12:55.960 --> 12:58.960
It's right here on the table.

12:58.960 --> 13:00.960
Right here.

13:00.960 --> 13:02.960
Look what we have.

13:02.960 --> 13:04.960
You know this, don't you?

13:04.960 --> 13:05.960
What is it?

13:05.960 --> 13:08.960
The big yellow box with the big blue letters on it?

13:08.960 --> 13:10.960
What does it say?

13:10.960 --> 13:13.960
Kix Crispy Corn Puffs.

13:13.960 --> 13:14.960
That's right.

13:14.960 --> 13:16.960
This is a new box, too.

13:16.960 --> 13:18.960
Look at this.

13:18.960 --> 13:25.960
Just ready to put on the pantry shelf so that it's there, right on the shelf when you want it for breakfast.

13:25.960 --> 13:26.960
Or for lunch.

13:26.960 --> 13:27.960
Or for supper.

13:27.960 --> 13:28.960
Sure.

13:28.960 --> 13:37.960
Say, wouldn't it be fun to come down to breakfast some morning and find a bowl of Kix that looks like this?

13:37.960 --> 13:41.960
Smiling right up at you.

13:41.960 --> 13:42.960
Look at that.

13:42.960 --> 13:45.960
Blueberries, two eyes, a nose, and a mouth.

13:45.960 --> 13:48.960
That mouth is really smiling.

13:48.960 --> 13:49.960
Isn't it?

13:49.960 --> 13:50.960
Yeah.

13:50.960 --> 13:55.960
And we have another bowl of Kix, too, right here.

13:55.960 --> 14:06.960
This one doesn't have a face on it, but look, it has four pieces of banana sticking up in the sides and a half of a fresh peach right in the middle.

14:06.960 --> 14:09.960
Doesn't that look delicious?

14:09.960 --> 14:12.960
Mmm, makes my mouth water.

14:12.960 --> 14:17.960
And we have a third bowl of Kix right here.

14:17.960 --> 14:18.960
Look at this.

14:18.960 --> 14:29.960
With sliced peaches all around the side of the dish, a blueberry between each slice, and the center is all filled with Kix.

14:29.960 --> 14:35.960
Say, those would be very, very good for breakfast, wouldn't they?

14:35.960 --> 14:36.960
Yes.

14:36.960 --> 14:38.960
And you know why they taste so good?

14:38.960 --> 14:39.960
Why?

14:39.960 --> 14:43.960
Because they're crisp, they're crunchy, they're delicious.

14:43.960 --> 14:51.960
They stay crisp to the last spoonful, and they have that wonderful corn flavor.

14:51.960 --> 14:52.960
That's right.

14:52.960 --> 14:59.960
So when you're ready for breakfast, you think about having a bowl of Kix, will you?

14:59.960 --> 15:02.960
They're good.

15:02.960 --> 15:07.960
Say, earlier this morning, we had lots of fun blowing bubbles, didn't we?

15:07.960 --> 15:19.960
And then, yes, we had lots of fun reading some poems and thinking about all those things, shadows and turtles and the moon.

15:19.960 --> 15:23.960
And you know the moon has a very different shape, doesn't it, sometimes?

15:23.960 --> 15:31.960
Sometimes it's a crescent, sometimes it gets bigger and thicker, and then sometimes it gets to be a real full moon.

15:31.960 --> 15:34.960
Well, there's some other things, too, that are lots of fun.

15:34.960 --> 15:40.960
And something else that I want to do that is lots and lots of fun, I'm not going to tell you what it is.

15:40.960 --> 15:43.960
No, no.

15:43.960 --> 15:48.960
Sometimes I do it, sometimes somebody else does it, sometimes your daddy might want to do it.

15:48.960 --> 15:52.960
Yes, it's fun to do in your lap, sometimes you want to do it on the table.

15:52.960 --> 15:54.960
And this time, I want to do it on the table.

15:54.960 --> 15:57.960
We've done lots of things on the table this morning, haven't we?

15:57.960 --> 16:01.960
Well, there isn't anything on this table.

16:01.960 --> 16:02.960
Not a thing.

16:02.960 --> 16:09.960
No tub, no pipes, no dishes, no books, nothing.

16:09.960 --> 16:11.960
What do you think we're going to do?

16:11.960 --> 16:14.960
Well, you can't do anything with just your hands and an empty table.

16:14.960 --> 16:19.960
You need something, and we do need something, and we're going to have something.

16:19.960 --> 16:23.960
And look what we have.

16:23.960 --> 16:24.960
What is this?

16:24.960 --> 16:26.960
What?

16:26.960 --> 16:28.960
Does it look like a napkin?

16:28.960 --> 16:30.960
Looks like a placemat?

16:30.960 --> 16:33.960
Looks like a small tablecloth?

16:33.960 --> 16:35.960
But you know what it really is?

16:35.960 --> 16:40.960
It's a handkerchief, just like the one your daddy has, or your granddaddy, or your uncle.

16:40.960 --> 16:42.960
Sure.

16:42.960 --> 16:45.960
It's a big, man-sized handkerchief.

16:45.960 --> 16:48.960
Did you ever play with just a handkerchief?

16:48.960 --> 16:49.960
Did you?

16:49.960 --> 16:58.960
Well, look, this is what we're going to do.

16:58.960 --> 17:02.960
You've seen things folded like that before.

17:02.960 --> 17:06.960
Why, of course you have.

17:06.960 --> 17:07.960
Makes a triangle.

17:07.960 --> 17:08.960
It really does.

17:08.960 --> 17:10.960
It makes a triangle.

17:10.960 --> 17:12.960
You know what I'm going to do with this triangle?

17:12.960 --> 17:13.960
Watch.

17:13.960 --> 17:15.960
Just with my hand, I haven't anything else.

17:15.960 --> 17:18.960
I'm going to put my hand under it like that.

17:18.960 --> 17:26.960
Then I'm going over and get this corner and poke it up here.

17:26.960 --> 17:28.960
Pull it up like that.

17:28.960 --> 17:30.960
See it?

17:30.960 --> 17:41.960
Then I'm going to take this corner and put it down under here and poke it up, pull it up.

17:41.960 --> 17:43.960
There we go.

17:43.960 --> 17:45.960
Just like that.

17:45.960 --> 17:48.960
Doesn't look like very much yet, does it?

17:48.960 --> 17:50.960
Put this corner under here.

17:50.960 --> 17:55.960
Now I'm going to pull it up a little more and pull this one up a little more.

17:55.960 --> 18:01.960
Look what we have.

18:01.960 --> 18:02.960
What do we have?

18:02.960 --> 18:06.960
What do you think it is?

18:06.960 --> 18:09.960
A rabbit with floppy ears.

18:09.960 --> 18:15.960
Sure, his ears don't know which way to flop first.

18:15.960 --> 18:18.960
Oh, there's so many things you can do with a hat stitcher.

18:18.960 --> 18:21.960
Let's start again.

18:21.960 --> 18:23.960
We'll leave it folded this time.

18:23.960 --> 18:24.960
Just like that.

18:24.960 --> 18:26.960
See?

18:26.960 --> 18:30.960
See, it's folded again just the way it was before.

18:30.960 --> 18:33.960
I'm going to turn it this way for a minute.

18:33.960 --> 18:47.960
I'm going to start rolling at this corner and roll it and roll it and roll it.

18:47.960 --> 19:05.960
I'm going to start at this corner and roll it and roll it and keep on rolling.

19:05.960 --> 19:10.960
I'm going to roll both of them.

19:10.960 --> 19:14.960
Well, what do you think this is going to be?

19:14.960 --> 19:16.960
You don't know?

19:16.960 --> 19:18.960
You're not sure?

19:18.960 --> 19:20.960
What does it look like?

19:20.960 --> 19:21.960
What?

19:21.960 --> 19:23.960
Just two?

19:23.960 --> 19:24.960
Well, it does.

19:24.960 --> 19:27.960
It looks just like a rolled up handkerchief, you're right.

19:27.960 --> 19:30.960
I'm going to bend it over this way.

19:30.960 --> 19:36.960
Get that right there and take this one and pull it underneath.

19:36.960 --> 19:41.960
I'm going to hold that part like that and then I'm going to pull.

19:41.960 --> 19:45.960
Sometimes they pull easily and sometimes they don't.

19:45.960 --> 19:47.960
It depends sometimes.

19:47.960 --> 19:52.960
Well, look what came out.

19:52.960 --> 19:54.960
Yes, look at this.

19:54.960 --> 19:57.960
Who's in here in a cradle, little hammock?

19:57.960 --> 19:59.960
Two dolls, one.

19:59.960 --> 20:01.960
They're very close together.

20:01.960 --> 20:03.960
Yes, but there are two of them, look.

20:03.960 --> 20:09.960
And you can swing, see, just like that.

20:09.960 --> 20:13.960
Playing just with a handkerchief and making things.

20:13.960 --> 20:16.960
You pull it right out again, what do you have?

20:16.960 --> 20:18.960
Just your handkerchief.

20:18.960 --> 20:19.960
That's right.

20:19.960 --> 20:23.960
Just a large-sized handkerchief.

20:23.960 --> 20:27.960
Say, you know what I think would be lots of fun?

20:27.960 --> 20:34.960
When your daddy or granddaddy or mother or uncle or someone comes home tonight,

20:34.960 --> 20:42.960
I think it would be lots of fun for you to ask him or her to make out of a handkerchief

20:42.960 --> 20:47.960
a rabbit or a cradle with two dolls in it.

20:47.960 --> 20:49.960
Will you do that?

20:49.960 --> 20:53.960
What will you do if they say they don't know how to do it?

20:53.960 --> 20:55.960
Will you?

20:55.960 --> 20:59.960
You'll be able to help them, show them how to do it?

20:59.960 --> 21:01.960
Will you really?

21:01.960 --> 21:02.960
Good.

21:02.960 --> 21:06.960
Oh, because I think they'll be surprised and pleased.

21:06.960 --> 21:09.960
It's lots and lots and lots of fun.

21:09.960 --> 21:11.960
So you try it.

21:11.960 --> 21:13.960
Maybe you'll think of something else to make.

21:13.960 --> 21:17.960
Not a rabbit, not dolls, but a cradle.

21:17.960 --> 21:19.960
That would be fun.

21:19.960 --> 21:21.960
You know what I want to do now?

21:21.960 --> 21:27.960
Oh, this is what I want to do.

21:27.960 --> 21:33.960
I haven't played with it for a long, long time, and oh, do I like it.

21:33.960 --> 21:35.960
I certainly do.

21:35.960 --> 21:38.960
What do you do with your ball, your big ball?

21:38.960 --> 21:43.960
You roll it, and sometimes you throw it into something.

21:43.960 --> 21:45.960
You know what I want to do with this one?

21:45.960 --> 21:51.960
Watch.

21:51.960 --> 21:53.960
Just like dropping it.

21:53.960 --> 21:56.960
Look, when you bounce it, just let go.

21:56.960 --> 21:59.960
Here you have both hands, and you just let go.

21:59.960 --> 22:04.960
Watch.

22:04.960 --> 22:05.960
Isn't that fun?

22:05.960 --> 22:07.960
Where would you bounce a ball?

22:07.960 --> 22:09.960
Your ball won't bounce.

22:09.960 --> 22:10.960
Why?

22:10.960 --> 22:18.960
Oh, it's a big beach ball, and it has a place at one end that makes it so it won't bounce.

22:18.960 --> 22:20.960
But this is a rubber ball.

22:20.960 --> 22:21.960
You don't have to have a big ball to bounce it.

22:21.960 --> 22:23.960
You can have any size ball.

22:23.960 --> 22:31.960
Yes, you can have a little ball or you can have a big ball, but remember that if you miss it, a ball rolls away, doesn't it?

22:31.960 --> 22:34.960
And that's what you have to remember, to hold on to it.

22:34.960 --> 22:40.960
If it rolls away out into the street, you wouldn't run after it, would you?

22:40.960 --> 22:41.960
No.

22:41.960 --> 22:51.960
And today, remember, is Friday, so there's no ding-dong school tomorrow and no ding-dong school on Saturday.

22:51.960 --> 22:52.960
That's right.

22:52.960 --> 22:57.960
But there will be again on Monday, and you're going to have a fine weekend, aren't you?

22:57.960 --> 22:58.960
Good for you.

22:58.960 --> 22:59.960
You just have a good one.

22:59.960 --> 23:01.960
Do you know what time it is?

23:01.960 --> 23:03.960
It is.

23:03.960 --> 23:04.960
I'm going to take you home with me.

23:04.960 --> 23:05.960
Where are they?

23:05.960 --> 23:07.960
Do you remember?

23:07.960 --> 23:12.960
All right, because Miss Helen is all ready to play, and you remember where to go.

23:12.960 --> 23:40.960
All right, go get them.

23:40.960 --> 23:42.960
Thank you, boys and girls.

23:42.960 --> 23:44.960
Thank you very much.

23:44.960 --> 23:52.960
You may go play now, and I hope you have a very happy weekend, because I want to talk with whoever is at home with you now.

23:52.960 --> 23:57.960
Say, we had lots of fun this morning playing with a large-sized man's handkerchief.

23:57.960 --> 24:00.960
Do you know the many, many things you can do with a handkerchief?

24:00.960 --> 24:03.960
Well, we made a rabbit's head.

24:03.960 --> 24:09.960
We made a cradle with two little dolls in it this morning, and I'm sure you'll think of many, many other things that can be made.

24:09.960 --> 24:23.960
I've suggested really very quietly to the children that whoever comes home tonight, that they ask that person to make, out of a handkerchief, a rabbit's head or a cradle.

24:23.960 --> 24:27.960
And if they can't do it, I think the children might be able to show them how.

24:27.960 --> 24:35.960
It's a little tricky at first, and we'll do it again one day next week, just in case they haven't gotten it, just in case they don't remember how to roll it.

24:35.960 --> 24:41.960
We'll try it again, but just think what fun it would be to ask someone to make one for you and not have them know how.

24:41.960 --> 24:44.960
Oh, maybe even if you know, you'll pretend that you don't.

24:44.960 --> 24:46.960
They would love that.

24:46.960 --> 24:55.960
And, of course, playing with a handkerchief is the kind of thing you can do in the car, in an airplane, on a train, just anywhere, on a picnic, you know, when things get just a little bit restless.

24:55.960 --> 25:02.960
Say, also this morning, we had some poetry, some lovely poems that the children I know would like to learn as they go along.

25:02.960 --> 25:08.960
The two of them were by Rachel Lindsay, The Turtle and The Moon's and North Wind's Cookie.

25:08.960 --> 25:12.960
And then we had a third one, The Shadow, My Shadow, by Robert Louis Stevenson.

25:12.960 --> 25:15.960
And it was lots and lots of fun.

25:15.960 --> 25:28.960
We had something else, too, that's interesting and lots of fun, which you know about, because you know today is our day with General Mills' Make Kix, the ready-to-eat, crispy corn puff cereal.

25:28.960 --> 25:38.960
And, of course, one of the reasons that we enjoy Kix so much is because it is not only ready to eat, but it's so crisp and so delicious.

25:38.960 --> 25:43.960
And it stays crisp, as you know, clear through to the last spoonful.

25:43.960 --> 25:50.960
And this is the family-sized package, which means if you get a package like this, it'll last at least a couple of days.

25:50.960 --> 25:57.960
And, of course, Kix are delicious plain, with sugar and milk, or combined with fruit.

25:57.960 --> 26:03.960
And I was just thinking, you know, sometimes it's lots of fun to surprise children with a bowl of Kix for breakfast.

26:03.960 --> 26:10.960
So that when they actually come down, sit down at the table, the fun, the surprise of seeing a bowl all arranged for them.

26:10.960 --> 26:20.960
Look at this one, which is a bowl of Kix with four pieces of banana around it and a half of a fresh peach in the middle.

26:20.960 --> 26:30.960
Now, the combination of a soft banana and a delicious, fresh peach with crisp corn puffs is really a delightful combination.

26:30.960 --> 26:34.960
And that's just the kind of thing that you want to start your day off, isn't it?

26:34.960 --> 26:39.960
We have another one here, too, that I've seen children enjoy so many times, and that is a face.

26:39.960 --> 26:42.960
This one happens to be made out of blueberries.

26:42.960 --> 26:50.960
And the face is always a smile, because the mouth turns up, and they really just chuckle when they sit down and see this one.

26:50.960 --> 26:57.960
And then, of course, another combination is a good one, and that is this one where you put the fruit around the bowl,

26:57.960 --> 27:03.960
like the fresh peaches, a little blueberry, or any kind of berries between, just whatever you have in the house.

27:03.960 --> 27:06.960
And then put the Kix right in the center.

27:06.960 --> 27:08.960
See, isn't that even a different way, isn't it?

27:08.960 --> 27:15.960
And I think that all these nice, warm mornings, when you want something very refreshing for breakfast,

27:15.960 --> 27:21.960
what could be better than to sit down to a bowl of Kix, crispy corn puffs?

27:21.960 --> 27:28.960
They not only are crisp and delicious to taste, but they also have minerals, vitamins, and proteins,

27:28.960 --> 27:33.960
just what we need to make it possible for us to have a good day.

27:33.960 --> 27:35.960
Get a box for the weekend.

27:35.960 --> 27:37.960
You'll be very glad you did, I'm sure.

27:37.960 --> 27:45.960
Well, as you know, here it is, the end of July, which means half of the summer is over.

27:45.960 --> 27:49.960
Really? Doesn't seem possible, but the days just have a way of flying by.

27:49.960 --> 27:57.960
And if you have planned some things with your children that you want to do with them or for them, or they with you,

27:57.960 --> 28:05.960
it would be a good time just about now to take inventory, wouldn't it, and see what were the plans that really were carried out,

28:05.960 --> 28:10.960
and what plans did we find that we couldn't carry out, that it was impractical,

28:10.960 --> 28:13.960
or for one reason or another, we no longer can do it.

28:13.960 --> 28:17.960
Well, that's pretty important to sit down now and talk it over with children

28:17.960 --> 28:22.960
and see what things we are going to do during the month of August and the early part of September.

28:22.960 --> 28:29.960
And I think that children need to know, they need to enjoy the planning and the evaluation of the plan.

28:29.960 --> 28:35.960
So many times we plan things, and then we forget later to evaluate them.

28:35.960 --> 28:39.960
And of course, if children are going to make plans for themselves,

28:39.960 --> 28:46.960
one of the important things for them to learn is to later evaluate the plan and see how well it worked.

28:46.960 --> 28:49.960
And if it didn't work, why?

28:49.960 --> 28:51.960
Well, it's time for me to say goodbye.

28:51.960 --> 28:54.960
Do have a happy weekend and enjoy your children.

28:54.960 --> 28:57.960
Well, I'll see you on Monday. Goodbye.

28:57.960 --> 29:26.960
♪♪♪

29:27.960 --> 29:32.960
♪♪♪

